; ======================================================================
;  hw_gpio.pi4 - the Pi 4 HwGpio* backend. THE ONE FILE THAT KNOWS THE
;  BCM2711'S GPIO ENCODING; the core above it knows only #HW_* codes.
;
;  This is the board side of the HwGpio* seam declared in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi.
;  Anvil/Core/gpio_cmd.pbi (the `gpio` command) calls these names and never
;  touches a register or a raw FSEL code; this file is thin wrappers over
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4, whose header carries the register-level
;  reasoning (the $FE200000 base, the .l accesses, the BCM2711-only pull
;  registers, and the out-of-order ALT4/ALT5 codes).
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER, and it is not arbitrary:
;    * RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio.pi4  BEFORE this file - it defines PinModeGet,
;      DigitalRead, PinPullGet and the #PIN_*/#PULL_* raw codes this file
;      translates. A library never includes a library (house rule 10), so
;      board.pi4 lists both, gpio.pi4 first.
;    * Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi          BEFORE this file - it defines the #HW_*
;      vocabulary these wrappers return.
;
;  WHY TRANSLATE AT ALL, RATHER THAN RETURN THE RAW CODES. Because the raw
;  codes are the part the core must not learn. Lib/gpio.pi4's FSEL numbers
;  are the silicon's (Table 64): input is 000, output is 001, and the ALT
;  codes are OUT OF ORDER - ALT4 is 3 and ALT5 is 2, not 8 and 9. A core
;  that rendered a raw FSEL code would either carry that BCM2711 quirk
;  (a chip fact in chip-free code) or render ALT4 and ALT5 wrong. So the
;  ALT numbering is undone HERE, where the table is already documented, and
;  the core is handed #HW_GPIO_ALT plus a plain alt number 0..5.
;
;  #CAP_GPIO IS 1 ON THIS BOARD, so this real backend is what runs. A board
;  that declares #CAP_GPIO = 0 provides a stub hw_gpio (every wrapper
;  returning the "bad"/-1 answer) and RequireCap in Anvil/Core/gpio_cmd.pbi
;  refuses the command before any of these are reached - so these wrappers
;  never have to defend against being called on a board with no GPIO.
;
;  NOT HARDWARE-VERIFIED. Lib/gpio.pi4's header says its procedures have
;  never driven a real pin (checked 2026-08-27); wrapping them does not
;  change that. The read path the `gpio` command uses (mode, level, pull)
;  is read-only and safe to run on a live board; the write seam below
;  (HwGpioMode/HwGpioWrite/HwGpioToggle) is provided for the fuller gpio
;  family and inherits Lib/gpio.pi4's caveats about read-modify-write not
;  being multi-core safe.
; ======================================================================

; ======================================================================
;  BCM2712 (Pi 5, -t pi5, #PMF_CHIP = 2712) - THE SAME SEAM OVER RP1.
;
;  On the Pi 5 the 40-pin header is not the SoC's GPIO at all; it is the
;  RP1 southbridge's bank 0, a different peripheral IP. So on 2712 these
;  wrappers sit on RaspberryPi4/Lib/gpio_rp1.pi4 (Rule 30's "different IP
;  gets a thin host file"), which the board lists INSTEAD of gpio.pi4
;  for the pin calls. The Pi 4 branch below the CompilerElse is untouched
;  and is what -t pi4 compiles, byte for byte.
;
;  WHAT CHANGES IN THE TRANSLATION, and why each line is here:
;    * Mode: RP1 splits "which function" (IO_BANK0 CTRL FUNCSEL) from "which
;      way SIO drives" (SYS_RIO0 OE). FUNCSEL 5 is the plain-GPIO function
;      (pinctrl-rp1.c:101); only then does OE decide input vs output. Any
;      other table column 0..8 is an alternate function, and its NUMBER is
;      the column - pinctrl-rp1.c's PIN() rows list a0..a8 in order, no
;      BCM2711-style out-of-order codes to undo.
;    * FUNCSEL >= 9 is RP1_FSEL_NONE (pinctrl-rp1.c:102-103; $1F is the
;      hardware's "no function"). The pin has no function at all, which is
;      neither input, output nor a nameable alternate, so ModeGet answers
;      -1 and the `gpio` report prints "??" rather than a plausible word.
;    * Pull: RP1's field is 1 = DOWN, 2 = UP - the REVERSE of the BCM2711
;      (gpio_rp1.pi4's header). Mapped by name, both directions, so the
;      reversal lives in exactly one pair of Selects.
;    * Extent: gpio_rp1.pi4 drives bank 0 only (RP1 GPIO 0..27), which is
;      exactly the header, so Count and UserMax are both that bank.
;    * Reserved: the console. The first Pi 5 Anvil boot was captured from
;      RP1 UART0 on header pins 8/10 (vault HARDWARE-BRINGUP.md, 2026-09-26
;      18:09), which are RP1 GPIO 14/15 on FUNCSEL 4 (pinctrl-rp1.c:496-497,
;      column a4 = uart0). The Pi 5 radio is on BCM2712 SDIO2 pins, not RP1
;      bank 0, so it has nothing to reserve here.
;
;  DESK-PROOF ONLY (tools/a64/gpio_rp1_check.py). Silicon owed.
; ======================================================================
CompilerIf #PMF_CHIP = 2712

; Every RP1 GPIO line, banks 0..2 - the lines gpio_rp1.pi4 can reach, so
; the advertised count and the reachable set agree (ruling 2026-09-26:
; match the Pi 4, which advertises all 58 BCM2711 lines with UserMax 27).
Procedure.i HwGpioCount()
  ProcedureReturn #RP1_GPIO_LAST + 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioUserMax()
  ProcedureReturn #RP1_PIN_MAX
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioModeGet(pin.i)
  Define f.i
  f = Rp1FuncSelGet(pin)
  If f < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If f = #RP1_FUNCSEL_GPIO
    If Rp1PinModeGet(pin) = 1
      ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_OUT
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_IN
  EndIf
  If f <= 8
    ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_ALT
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioAltGet(pin.i)
  Define f.i
  f = Rp1FuncSelGet(pin)
  If f < 0 Or f = #RP1_FUNCSEL_GPIO Or f > 8
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn f
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioLevelGet(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn Rp1DigitalRead(pin)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioPullGet(pin.i)
  Select Rp1PinPullGet(pin)
    Case #RP1_PULL_NONE
      ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_NONE
    Case #RP1_PULL_UP
      ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_UP
    Case #RP1_PULL_DOWN
      ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_DOWN
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; #HW_GPIO_ALT is refused for the same reason as on the Pi 4: "some
; alternate" is not a whole instruction.
Procedure.i HwGpioMode(pin.i, mode.i)
  If mode = #HW_GPIO_IN
    ProcedureReturn Rp1PinMode(pin, 0)
  EndIf
  If mode = #HW_GPIO_OUT
    ProcedureReturn Rp1PinMode(pin, 1)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioWrite(pin.i, level.i)
  If level = 0
    ProcedureReturn Rp1DigitalWrite(pin, #RP1_PIN_LOW)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn Rp1DigitalWrite(pin, #RP1_PIN_HIGH)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioToggle(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn Rp1PinToggle(pin)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioReservedReason(pin.i)
  If pin = 14 Or pin = 15
    ProcedureReturn "the serial console this prompt is arriving on: RP1 UART0 is on GPIO 14 and 15 (header pins 8 and 10), and driving either would cut the console mid-command"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

CompilerElse

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Extent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioCount()
  ; Every GPIO line the BCM2711 has - 58, banks 0..2 (Lib/gpio.pi4's
  ; #PIN_COUNT, from BCM2711 ARM Peripherals section 5.1).
  ProcedureReturn #PIN_COUNT
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioUserMax()
  ; The highest pin that reaches the 40-pin header - GPIO0..27 are the
  ; user pins (Pi 4 product datasheet section 5.1, "28x user GPIO");
  ; 28..57 are real die lines used for on-board functions. The `gpio`
  ; command reports up to here by default so it does not invite an
  ; operator to poke a pin that is wired to the SD card or the PHY.
  ProcedureReturn 27
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Mode - the pin's function, normalised to the #HW_GPIO_* vocabulary.
;
;  PinModeGet returns the raw 3-bit FSEL code (0..7) or -1 for a bad pin.
;  0 is input, 1 is output, everything else is some alternate function.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioModeGet(pin.i)
  Define m.i
  m = PinModeGet(pin)
  If m < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If m = #PIN_INPUT
    ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_IN
  EndIf
  If m = #PIN_OUTPUT
    ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_OUT
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_ALT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwGpioAltGet - the alternate-function NUMBER (0..5) for an ALT pin, or
;  -1 for a bad pin or a pin that is a plain input/output.
;
;  THIS IS WHERE THE OUT-OF-ORDER TABLE IS UNDONE. Lib/gpio.pi4's
;  #PIN_ALT0..#PIN_ALT5 are the raw codes 4,5,6,7,3,2 (Table 64, in that
;  order on purpose). Mapping each back to its human ALT number is a
;  Select over those six constants, so the mapping is one place and a
;  reviewer checks six lines against the six constants - the same shape the
;  library uses to hold the table in the first place.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioAltGet(pin.i)
  Define m.i
  m = PinModeGet(pin)
  If m < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  Select m
    Case #PIN_ALT0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    Case #PIN_ALT1
      ProcedureReturn 1
    Case #PIN_ALT2
      ProcedureReturn 2
    Case #PIN_ALT3
      ProcedureReturn 3
    Case #PIN_ALT4
      ProcedureReturn 4
    Case #PIN_ALT5
      ProcedureReturn 5
  EndSelect
  ; input or output - not an alternate function.
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Level and pull - already in the portable shape.
;
;  DigitalRead returns 0/1/-1 directly (#HW_ level codes ARE 0 and 1, and
;  -1 is the shared bad-pin answer), so it needs no translation. PinPullGet
;  returns #PULL_NONE/#PULL_UP/#PULL_DOWN whose values happen to equal the
;  #HW_PULL_* codes, but they are mapped explicitly rather than passed
;  through: the two enumerations are defined in different files for
;  different reasons and a future divergence in either must not silently
;  mislabel a pull. -1 (bad pin) and 3 (the reserved field value
;  Lib/gpio.pi4 can report) both fall through to -1 here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioLevelGet(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn DigitalRead(pin)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioPullGet(pin.i)
  Define p.i
  p = PinPullGet(pin)
  If p = #PULL_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_NONE
  EndIf
  If p = #PULL_UP
    ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_UP
  EndIf
  If p = #PULL_DOWN
    ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_DOWN
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The write seam - for the fuller gpio family (input/set/clear/toggle),
;  not the read-only `gpio` status command wired today. Thin wrappers, so
;  the family lands against the same seam the report already uses.
;
;  HwGpioMode takes a #HW_GPIO_* code and turns it into a Lib/gpio.pi4
;  call. #HW_GPIO_ALT is refused here (returns 0): "make this pin some
;  alternate function" is not a whole instruction - WHICH alternate is,
;  and that is PinAlt(pin, n)'s job, which a later HwGpioAlt(pin, n) will
;  expose. Refusing the ambiguous case rather than guessing an ALT is the
;  same discipline PinMode uses when it refuses a mode outside 0..7.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioMode(pin.i, mode.i)
  If mode = #HW_GPIO_IN
    ProcedureReturn PinMode(pin, #PIN_INPUT)
  EndIf
  If mode = #HW_GPIO_OUT
    ProcedureReturn PinMode(pin, #PIN_OUTPUT)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioWrite(pin.i, level.i)
  ProcedureReturn DigitalWrite(pin, level)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioToggle(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn PinToggle(pin)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwGpioReservedReason - the pins the BOARD ITSELF is using, that the
;  `gpio` write subcommands must refuse to reconfigure.
;
;  Returns 0 for a pin that is free to drive, or the address of a
;  lowercase fragment naming the on-board function that owns the pin. The
;  fragment reads after "GPIO n is " and the core prints it with
;  UartWriteStr, exactly as RequireCap prints its *reason - so the core
;  never learns WHICH pins these are. This is the board's list because the
;  wiring is the board's fact.
;
;  READING ANY PIN STAYS ALLOWED - this gates writes only. The status
;  report deliberately reports these pins (that is how you SEE that the
;  console is on ALT0 and the radio bus is up); what is refused is
;  RE-configuring them out from under the running machine.
;
;  HOW THE LIST WAS DERIVED - from how the Pi 4's own drivers mux their
;  pins, not from a web page:
;
;    GPIO 14, 15  the serial console. RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4's
;                 uart_MuxPins() muxes exactly these two to ALT0 (TXD0/
;                 RXD0); its header cites bcm2711-peripherals.pdf Table 171
;                 and the device tree's uart0_gpio14 group. This is the pair
;                 that carries this very prompt, so driving either one takes
;                 the console down between one command and the next.
;
;    GPIO 34..39  the on-board Wi-Fi/Bluetooth radio. RaspberryPi4/Lib/
;                 sdio.pi4 (header line 8, and sdio_MuxPins) drives the
;                 CYW43455 - soldered down - over an SDIO bus muxed onto
;                 these six lines, all in GPFSEL3. Reconfiguring one breaks
;                 the radio's bus.
;
;    the micro-SD card  is NOT here, and that is a real finding rather than
;                 an omission. RaspberryPi4/Lib/emmc.pi4 (the "EMMC2 NEEDS
;                 NO PIN MUXING" note, its lines 202-206) records that the
;                 Pi 4's card slot is EMMC2 on a DEDICATED socket with no
;                 pinctrl and no BCM2711 GPIO behind it - so there are no
;                 SD/eMMC pins on the 0..57 map to reserve. The legacy
;                 SD0/SDIO on GPIO 48..53 that older Pis used for the card
;                 is not the Pi 4's card path.
;
;    GPIO 30..33  the same radio's Bluetooth UART. RaspberryPi4/Lib/
;                 bt_uart.pi4 muxes these four to ALT3 (PL011 CTS/RTS/TXD/
;                 RXD, bcm283x.dtsi 259-268) when `bt up` opens the link.
;                 Until the console leaves the PL011, `bt up` refuses on
;                 this board and nothing muxes them; they are reserved
;                 anyway, because they are the radio's wires, not the
;                 user's, and a write to one mid-link would break it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioReservedReason(pin.i)
  If pin = 14 Or pin = 15
    ProcedureReturn "the serial console this prompt is arriving on: the PL011 UART is muxed onto GPIO 14 and 15, and driving either would cut the console mid-command"
  EndIf
  If pin >= 34 And pin <= 39
    ProcedureReturn "part of the on-board Wi-Fi and Bluetooth radio's SDIO bus: the CYW43455 is soldered down on GPIO 34 to 39"
  EndIf
  If pin >= 30 And pin <= 33
    ProcedureReturn "the on-board radio's Bluetooth UART: the CYW43455's HCI link is on GPIO 30 to 33"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
CompilerEndIf
